<% content_for :title, "The Big Picture & Bipolar | Bipolar Sober" %>

<main class="big-picture-page">

  <!-- HERO -->
  <section class="big-picture-hero">
    <div class="big-picture-hero-content">

      <p class="big-picture-eyebrow">
        BIPOLAR SOBER / BIPOLAR / PERSPECTIVES / THE BIG PICTURE
      </p>

      <h1>
        <span>THE</span>
        <span>BIG</span>
        <span>PICTURE.</span>
      </h1>

      <p class="big-picture-intro">
        DIFFERENT LENSES.<br>
        SAME COMPLICATED FUCKING HUMAN.
      </p>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 01 -->
  <section class="big-picture-section big-picture-pink">

    <div class="big-picture-section-heading">
      <p class="big-picture-number">
        01 / SO WHAT'S THE POINT?
      </p>

      <h2>
        NOBODY<br>
        GETS THE<br>
        WHOLE THING.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="big-picture-copy">

      <p class="big-picture-lead">
        Buddhism, Hinduism, Stoicism, CBT, DBT, and IFS
        come from very different places.
      </p>

      <p>
        Some are spiritual traditions. Some are philosophical.
        Some are forms of psychotherapy.
      </p>

      <p>
        They aren't interchangeable, and they aren't competing
        explanations for bipolar disorder.
      </p>

      <p>
        But each one can give you a different question to ask
        when your brain, emotions, behavior, or life gets complicated.
      </p>

      <blockquote>
        YOU DON'T NEED ONE LENS<br>
        TO EXPLAIN EVERYTHING.
      </blockquote>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 02 -->
  <section class="big-picture-section big-picture-yellow">

    <div class="big-picture-section-heading">
      <p class="big-picture-number">
        02 / BUDDHISM
      </p>

      <h2>
        THIS WILL<br>
        CHANGE.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="big-picture-copy">

      <p class="big-picture-lead">
        Buddhism reminds you that thoughts, emotions,
        urges, and mental states are changing experiences.
      </p>

      <p>
        Depression can feel permanent.
        Mania can feel like you've finally discovered the truth.
        Anger can feel like something must happen immediately.
      </p>

      <p>
        Buddhism offers a pause:
      </p>

      <div class="big-picture-lens">
        <span>BUDDHISM ASKS:</span>
        <h3>
          CAN I NOTICE THIS WITHOUT BECOMING IT?
        </h3>
      </div>

      <blockquote>
        EXPERIENCE THE STORM.<br>
        DON'T CONFUSE YOURSELF WITH THE STORM.
      </blockquote>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 03 -->
  <section class="big-picture-section big-picture-purple">

    <div class="big-picture-section-heading">
      <p class="big-picture-number">
        03 / HINDUISM
      </p>

      <h2>
        YOU ARE<br>
        MORE THAN<br>
        THIS STATE.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="big-picture-copy">

      <p class="big-picture-lead">
        Hindu traditions can offer another way of separating
        identity from the constantly changing contents of the mind.
      </p>

      <p>
        Energy changes. Thoughts change. Emotions change.
        Circumstances change.
      </p>

      <p>
        You can observe those changes without deciding that
        whichever state you're in right now defines everything you are.
      </p>

      <div class="big-picture-lens">
        <span>HINDUISM ASKS:</span>
        <h3>
          WHAT REMAINS WHEN MY CURRENT STATE CHANGES?
        </h3>
      </div>

      <blockquote>
        MY MIND IS CHANGING.<br>
        I AM STILL HERE.
      </blockquote>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 04 -->
  <section class="big-picture-section big-picture-black">

    <div class="big-picture-section-heading">
      <p class="big-picture-number">
        04 / STOICISM
      </p>

      <h2>
        WHAT'S<br>
        ACTUALLY<br>
        MINE?
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="big-picture-copy">

      <p class="big-picture-lead">
        Stoicism redirects attention away from controlling everything
        and toward what you can actually influence.
      </p>

      <p>
        You can't control having bipolar disorder.
        You can't guarantee another episode will never happen.
        You can't control everybody else's opinion of you.
      </p>

      <p>
        But you can work with what is available to you:
        preparation, treatment, boundaries, repair, asking for help,
        and the next useful decision.
      </p>

      <div class="big-picture-lens">
        <span>STOICISM ASKS:</span>
        <h3>
          WHAT PART OF THIS IS ACTUALLY MINE TO DO?
        </h3>
      </div>

      <blockquote>
        STOP WRESTLING THE UNCONTROLLABLE.<br>
        WORK WITH WHAT'S YOURS.
      </blockquote>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 05 -->
  <section class="big-picture-section big-picture-pink">

    <div class="big-picture-section-heading">
      <p class="big-picture-number">
        05 / CBT
      </p>

      <h2>
        CHECK THE<br>
        FUCKING<br>
        RECEIPTS.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="big-picture-copy">

      <p class="big-picture-lead">
        CBT reminds you that your first interpretation
        isn't automatically the whole story.
      </p>

      <p>
        Thoughts influence emotions.
        Emotions influence behavior.
        Behavior can reinforce thoughts.
      </p>

      <p>
        Instead of immediately believing the first story,
        CBT gives you tools for examining it.
      </p>

      <div class="big-picture-lens">
        <span>CBT ASKS:</span>
        <h3>
          WHAT'S THE EVIDENCE FOR WHAT MY BRAIN IS TELLING ME?
        </h3>
      </div>

      <blockquote>
        THOUGHTS MATTER.<br>
        THOUGHTS AREN'T AUTOMATICALLY FACTS.
      </blockquote>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 06 -->
  <section class="big-picture-section big-picture-yellow">

    <div class="big-picture-section-heading">
      <p class="big-picture-number">
        06 / DBT
      </p>

      <h2>
        HOLD<br>
        BOTH.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="big-picture-copy">

      <p class="big-picture-lead">
        DBT refuses to make you choose between acceptance and change.
      </p>

      <p>
        You can accept that something happened and want something different.
      </p>

      <p>
        Your feelings can be valid and your behavior can still be ineffective.
      </p>

      <p>
        You can be doing your best and still need to learn
        how to do something differently.
      </p>

      <div class="big-picture-lens">
        <span>DBT ASKS:</span>
        <h3>
          WHAT IF TWO THINGS ARE TRUE AT THE SAME TIME?
        </h3>
      </div>

      <blockquote>
        ACCEPT WHAT IS.<br>
        CHANGE WHAT YOU CAN.
      </blockquote>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 07 -->
  <section class="big-picture-section big-picture-purple">

    <div class="big-picture-section-heading">
      <p class="big-picture-number">
        07 / IFS
      </p>

      <h2>
        WHO'S<br>
        TALKING<br>
        RIGHT NOW?
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="big-picture-copy">

      <p class="big-picture-lead">
        IFS gives you language for the contradictions
        that can exist inside one person.
      </p>

      <p>
        A part of you wants stability.
        Another misses intensity.
      </p>

      <p>
        A part wants connection.
        Another wants everybody to fuck off.
      </p>

      <p>
        Instead of deciding one voice is the entire you,
        IFS invites curiosity about what each part is trying to do.
      </p>

      <div class="big-picture-lens">
        <span>IFS ASKS:</span>
        <h3>
          WHAT IS THIS PART OF ME TRYING TO PROTECT?
        </h3>
      </div>

      <blockquote>
        LISTEN TO THE PART.<br>
        DON'T AUTOMATICALLY HAND IT THE WHEEL.
      </blockquote>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 08 / ALL TOGETHER -->
  <section class="big-picture-section big-picture-black">

    <div class="big-picture-section-heading">
      <p class="big-picture-number">
        08 / PUT THEM TOGETHER
      </p>

      <h2>
        ONE<br>
        FUCKING<br>
        MOMENT.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="big-picture-copy">

      <p class="big-picture-lead">
        Say something happens and your internal world immediately
        catches fire.
      </p>

      <div class="big-picture-stack">

        <div>
          <span>BUDDHISM</span>
          <p>
            Notice what's happening without immediately becoming it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>HINDUISM</span>
          <p>
            Remember that this mental state isn't the entirety of you.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>STOICISM</span>
          <p>
            Separate what you can influence from what you can't.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>CBT</span>
          <p>
            Check whether your interpretation actually fits the evidence.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>DBT</span>
          <p>
            Validate the emotion while choosing an effective response.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>IFS</span>
          <p>
            Get curious about which part is loud and what it's protecting.
          </p>
        </div>

      </div>

      <p>
        You don't have to literally run through six systems
        every time somebody pisses you off.
      </p>

      <p>
        The point is that you have options.
      </p>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 09 / COMMON GROUND -->
  <section class="big-picture-section big-picture-pink">

    <div class="big-picture-section-heading">
      <p class="big-picture-number">
        09 / COMMON GROUND
      </p>

      <h2>
        NOTICE.<br>
        PAUSE.<br>
        CHOOSE.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="big-picture-copy">

      <p class="big-picture-lead">
        Strip away the different terminology and a few themes
        keep showing up.
      </p>

      <div class="big-picture-common">

        <div>
          <span>NOTICE</span>
          <p>
            Something is happening inside me.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>CREATE SPACE</span>
          <p>
            I don't have to react the instant I experience it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>GET CURIOUS</span>
          <p>
            What is this thought, feeling, urge, or part telling me?
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>CHECK REALITY</span>
          <p>
            What are the facts? What am I assuming?
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>ACCEPT</span>
          <p>
            What is already true about this moment?
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>CHOOSE</span>
          <p>
            What response fits my values and the reality I'm in?
          </p>
        </div>

      </div>

      <blockquote>
        THE PAUSE IS WHERE YOU GET OPTIONS.
      </blockquote>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 10 / BIPOLAR -->
  <section class="big-picture-section big-picture-yellow">

    <div class="big-picture-section-heading">
      <p class="big-picture-number">
        10 / AND BIPOLAR?
      </p>

      <h2>
        A LENS<br>
        ISN'T A<br>
        CURE.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="big-picture-copy">

      <p class="big-picture-lead">
        None of these perspectives makes bipolar disorder
        disappear.
      </p>

      <p>
        You cannot mindfulness your way out of mania.
        You cannot Stoic your way out of psychosis.
        You cannot CBT every biological component of a mood disorder
        into submission.
      </p>

      <p>
        These perspectives can be tools for understanding yourself,
        responding to emotions, examining thoughts, tolerating distress,
        making decisions, and living according to your values.
      </p>

      <p>
        They can exist alongside medication, therapy, psychiatric care,
        sleep, routines, support systems, sobriety, and whatever else
        belongs in your treatment and recovery plan.
      </p>

      <blockquote>
        USE THE TOOLS.<br>
        DON'T ASK THEM TO BE MAGIC.
      </blockquote>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- ENDING -->
  <section class="big-picture-ending">

    <div>

      <p class="big-picture-ending-small">
        MAYBE THERE ISN'T ONE PERFECT ANSWER.
      </p>

      <h2>
        BUILD YOUR<br>
        OWN FUCKING<br>
        <span>TOOLBOX.</span>
      </h2>

    </div>

    <div class="big-picture-ending-copy">

      <p>
        Take impermanence from Buddhism.
      </p>

      <p>
        Take perspective on the changing mind from Hinduism.
      </p>

      <p>
        Take agency from Stoicism.
      </p>

      <p>
        Take evidence-checking from CBT.
      </p>

      <p>
        Take acceptance and skills from DBT.
      </p>

      <p>
        Take curiosity toward your inner world from IFS.
      </p>

      <p>
        Keep what helps you understand yourself.
        Question what doesn't.
      </p>

      <p>
        No single perspective has to explain every side of you.
      </p>

      <%= link_to "← BACK TO PERSPECTIVES",
          bipolar_perspectives_path,
          class: "big-picture-back-button" %>

    </div>

  </section>

</main>